WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.944
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.244 --> 00:00:08.421
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.721 --> 00:00:16.250
Unit twenty-one: Analysing Poetry. Today you will offer alternative interpretations of poems with modal verbs.

4
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:19.063
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.105
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:24.649
Poem.

7
00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.925
Poem means a piece of writing arranged in lines, using rhythm and imagery.

8
00:00:30.517 --> 00:00:32.725
The poem is only twelve lines long.

9
00:00:33.317 --> 00:00:36.027
Your turn. Say: Poem.

10
00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:39.554
Poem.

11
00:00:40.317 --> 00:00:41.155
Stanza.

12
00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.450
Stanza means a group of lines forming a unit in a poem.

13
00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.189
The final stanza changes the mood.

14
00:00:48.783 --> 00:00:51.064
Your turn. Say: Stanza.

15
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:54.994
Stanza.

16
00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:56.658
Metaphor.

17
00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:01.287
Metaphor means describing one thing as another to suggest likeness.

18
00:01:01.883 --> 00:01:03.871
'Time is a thief' is a metaphor.

19
00:01:04.483 --> 00:01:06.625
Your turn. Say: Metaphor.

20
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:10.421
Metaphor.

21
00:01:11.183 --> 00:01:11.906
Rhythm.

22
00:01:12.917 --> 00:01:16.553
Rhythm means the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables.

23
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.033
The rhythm imitates a heartbeat.

24
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.919
Your turn. Say: Rhythm.

25
00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:25.547
Rhythm.

26
00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:27.122
Rhyme.

27
00:01:28.117 --> 00:01:31.544
Rhyme means the repetition of similar sounds at line endings.

28
00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.672
The poem avoids rhyme until the last couplet.

29
00:01:35.283 --> 00:01:37.611
Your turn. Say: Rhyme.

30
00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:41.352
Rhyme.

31
00:01:42.117 --> 00:01:43.118
Enjambment.

32
00:01:44.117 --> 00:01:47.939
Enjambment means a sentence running over the end of a line without a pause.

33
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.107
Enjambment creates a sense of breathlessness.

34
00:01:51.717 --> 00:01:54.462
Your turn. Say: Enjambment.

35
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.371
Enjambment.

36
00:01:59.117 --> 00:02:00.002
Voice.

37
00:02:01.017 --> 00:02:03.887
Voice means the speaker's personality in the poem.

38
00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:06.680
The voice is that of an elderly farmer.

39
00:02:07.283 --> 00:02:09.762
Your turn. Say: Voice.

40
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.706
Voice.

41
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:15.730
Interpretation.

42
00:02:16.717 --> 00:02:19.680
Interpretation means an explanation of a poem's meaning.

43
00:02:20.283 --> 00:02:22.631
Your interpretation is well supported.

44
00:02:23.217 --> 00:02:26.020
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

45
00:02:28.867 --> 00:02:30.135
Interpretation.

46
00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:34.689
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:40.176
Visal, the poem ends with the image of a tree losing its leaves. What do you make of it?

48
00:02:41.283 --> 00:02:51.227
The image might suggest loss, especially as the speaker has just mentioned her father. Or it could imply renewal, since leaves fall before spring.

49
00:02:52.317 --> 00:02:53.898
Which reading do you prefer?

50
00:02:54.983 --> 00:03:03.522
The enjambment in the last stanza might be the key. The line runs on into 'green again', so the poem could be pointing towards hope.

51
00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:06.535
Could the poet have intended both?

52
00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:16.107
She may well have. Great poems often hold two meanings in tension rather than forcing the reader to choose.

53
00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:18.721
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:19.583 --> 00:03:22.964
Grammar focus: Modal verbs for interpretation.

55
00:03:23.264 --> 00:03:24.463
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.136
The image might suggest loss, or it could imply renewal.

57
00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.142
The enjambment could be pointing towards hope.

58
00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:34.944
The poet may well have intended both meanings.

59
00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:47.829
Combine modals with interpretive verbs: suggest, imply, evoke, convey, hint at. 'May well' expresses a strong possibility; 'might have / could have + past participle' speculates about the poet's intentions.

60
00:03:48.583 --> 00:03:52.559
Be careful! Don't say: The image might suggests loss.

61
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.103
Say: The image might suggest loss.

62
00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:59.472
After a modal, use the base verb.

63
00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:02.733
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:03.533 --> 00:04:05.173
Reading a Poem Closely.

65
00:04:05.473 --> 00:04:11.060
Poetry compresses meaning into a small space, which is why every word may carry several possibilities.

66
00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:17.839
A single metaphor might evoke contradictory feelings, especially when its context is ambiguous.

67
00:04:18.833 --> 00:04:25.268
Form also contributes to meaning: enjambment could suggest urgency, while regular rhyme may convey order.

68
00:04:26.267 --> 00:04:32.771
Rather than searching for one correct answer, a skilled reader weighs alternative interpretations against the evidence.

69
00:04:33.767 --> 00:04:39.133
In this sense, analysing a poem is less like solving a puzzle than like holding a conversation.

70
00:04:40.233 --> 00:04:44.258
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:45.117 --> 00:04:47.128
The falling leaves blank loss.

72
00:04:47.428 --> 00:04:51.859
A, might suggest. B, might suggesting. C, might to suggest.

73
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:57.609
The answer is A: might suggest.

74
00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.156
The poet blank intended both meanings.

75
00:05:01.456 --> 00:05:05.121
A, may well. B, may well have. C, may have well.

76
00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:10.887
The answer is B: may well have.

77
00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:14.816
A sentence running over the end of a line is blank.

78
00:05:15.116 --> 00:05:18.804
A, rhyme. B, stanza. C, enjambment.

79
00:05:22.367 --> 00:05:24.389
The answer is C: enjambment.

80
00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:27.886
'Time is a thief' is an example of blank.

81
00:05:28.186 --> 00:05:32.153
A, a metaphor. B, a rhyme. C, a stanza.

82
00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.943
The answer is A: a metaphor.

83
00:05:39.083 --> 00:05:41.861
According to the reading, analysing a poem is like…

84
00:05:42.161 --> 00:05:47.184
A, solving a puzzle. B, holding a conversation. C, counting syllables.

85
00:05:50.733 --> 00:05:53.290
The answer is B: holding a conversation.

86
00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:57.861
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:58.717 --> 00:06:01.668
Why can one poem support several interpretations?

88
00:06:18.483 --> 00:06:19.879
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:58.319
One poem can support several interpretations because poetry relies on images and metaphors rather than direct statements. A single image, such as a river, might suggest time passing, or it could imply a journey or even freedom. Each reader also brings personal experiences, which may highlight different meanings. For example, a poem about leaving home could feel joyful to a student and painful to a migrant. Some teachers prefer one correct answer, but I believe multiple, well-supported readings make poetry richer.

90
00:06:58.619 --> 00:07:00.294
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:01.183 --> 00:07:05.821
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modal verbs for interpretation.

92
00:07:06.121 --> 00:07:10.631
Today's value is imagination. Imagine new ideas for a better future.

93
00:07:10.931 --> 00:07:13.641
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:13.941 --> 00:07:17.139
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

